<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19869608</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2131604</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>IMMUNOLOGICAL STUDIES IN RELATION TO THE SUPRARENAL GLAND </article-title><subtitle>IV. THE EFFECT OF REPEATED INJECTIONS OF EPINEPHRINE ON THE HEMOLYSIN FORMATION IN SUPRASENALECTOMIZED RATS</subtitle></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Perla</surname><given-names>David</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Marmorston-Gottesman</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x37686f0N0x3c08280">From the Division of Laboratories, Montefiore Hospital, New York</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>30</day><month>6</month><year>1929</year></pub-date><volume>50</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>87</fpage><lpage>92</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>3</day><month>5</month><year>1929</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1929, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Epinephrine diluted with physiological salt solution or with isotonic glucose solutions and injected in amounts of 0.2 mg. per kilo twice daily into suprarenalectomized rats raises their antibody forming capacity. This result is not entirely dependent on the effect of the diluting fluid. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>